 "finished_by_agent": true,
 "finish_summary": "I characterized the design point first: a logic-free floor test (registered mux only) reaches WNS 7.83 ns, so the fixed overhead (input delay + hold mux + setup) is ~2.17 ns and the entire 32-bit adder contributes only ~0.26 ns of the 2.51 ns critical path \u2014 the whole optimization headroom is ~2.6 score points, and area is worth 10x less per unit (1000 um2 = 1 pt). I then evaluated 16 RTL variants with the cheap estimator: Kogge-Stone, Sklansky, Brent-Kung (Yosys $lcu replica), radix-2 pair-leaf trees, OR-propagate (low input-cap) leaves, Ling-style forms, 8/16-bit carry-select, and several output-stage rewrites including a Shannon expansion that folds the final sum-XOR and the valid/reset mux into one gate. The Shannon output stage is a real win on explicit trees (+0.11 ns on Brent-Kung), but Yosys/ABC's native $add mapping produces a carry tree that is faster than any hand-built tree by more than that margin, and the trick cannot be applied to $add without an extra XOR to recover the carries. Two place_and_route runs confirmed the estimator ranking: Sklansky+Shannon (area 652, WNS 7.4667) scored 74.015, below the baseline's 746 um2 / 7.48722 ns / 74.126 which I re-verified as my final result. No variant reached an estimated score above the baseline, so further submissions were unlikely to help; the original formulation is kept as the best verified design.",
